      "content": "While it's unlikely that the chair has hidden sensors for employer tracking, it's possible that it has standard health monitoring features. Many modern ergonomic chairs come equipped with built-in sensors to track user activity, posture, and other health metrics. These features can provide valuable insights for users to adjust their working habits for better comfort and productivity, and may even be set to collect data for an extended period, which can raise concerns about user consent. Many companies also use data from similar devices for employee health and wellness research.",
